      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed that the prev check needs `!!vmg-\u003efile`: with B10\u0027s `!vma_is_anonymous(prev)`, a private /dev/zero `prev` skipped the slice comparison, so an `mprotect()` or `madvise()` merge could leave the pages of `middle` at the wrong slice.\n\nI\u0027m worried about the address-based slice, though. As far as I can see, the rest of the series maps private /dev/zero by offset, like any file. `mm_mmap_slice_off()` gives it `vm_slice_off \u003d pgoff \u0026 P3S_SLICE_MASK`, and `vma_slice_offset()` only uses the address when there\u0027s neither `vm_ops` nor `vm_file` (as your aosp/4367945 comment says). `do_anonymous_page()` maps that slice through `vma_pte_add_slice()`, and aosp/4367981 keeps it across `mremap()`. So I think the comment and the check are both off.\n\nFor example, `mmap(NULL, 8KB, ..., MAP_PRIVATE, zero_fd, 4KB)` gets a 16KB-aligned address with `vm_slice_off` 1. Growing it in place with `mremap()` goes through `vma_merge_extend()`, where the continuing slice (3) no longer matches the address slice (2). So `expand_vma_in_place()` now fails with `-ENOMEM`, where it succeeded before this patch. A VM test shows it: on the top of this series, growing such a mapping from 8KB to 12KB with `mremap()` fails with `ENOMEM`, also with `MREMAP_MAYMOVE`, while our b10 grows the same mapping in place. Conversely, a faulted range that `mremap()` moves to another phase still merges at the wrong slice into a /dev/zero `prev` whose slices follow its addresses. I think a later fault in that 16KB window can then reuse the slice that the moved page still maps.\n\nEvery caller already sets `vmg-\u003eslice_off` to the slice of the proposed range (`VMG_MMAP_STATE()`, `VMG_VMA_STATE()`, and `vma_mremap_slice_off()` in `copy_vma()`). It also equals `vma_slice_off(middle)` when an existing range merges. Anonymous memory has `is_file` false, so its slice isn\u0027t compared. Would it work for you to drop `vmg_new_range_slice()` and only change `is_file`?\n\n```\n\treturn vma_can_merge_offsets(vmg-\u003eprev, vmg-\u003epgoff, vmg-\u003eslice_off,\n\t\t\t\t     vmg-\u003estart, vmg_is_p3s_4k(vmg), !!vmg-\u003efile);\n```\n\nThe `!vmg-\u003eprev` check can go too, since `can_vma_merge_left()` checks it before we get here.",
