      "side": 1,
      "message": "This one predates your patch (it comes from our aosp/4366900), but this patch reworks the same check, and I think the next side has the mirror problem. `vma_is_anonymous()` is also true for private /dev/zero (and private ashmem), so such a `next` is compared in 4KB pages. Its `vm_pgoff` counts 16KB file pages, though, as `vma_linear_page_index()` and `p3s_anon_rmap_skip()` expect.\n\nFor example, take a 4KB process with one /dev/zero fd and `MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_FIXED`. Map offset 64KB, 16KB long, at A + 16KB (`vm_pgoff` 4) and touch it, then map offset 0, 16KB long, at A. The check sees 4 \u003d\u003d 0 + (16KB \u003e\u003e 12) and merges. The merged VMA (`vm_pgoff` 0) expects file page 1 at A + 16KB, while the folio there has index 4. rmap then looks for it at A + 64KB, outside the VMA, so reclaim and migration can\u0027t unmap it. A VM test shows this on both our b10 and the top of this series: the two mappings merge into one VMA at offset 0, `MADV_PAGEOUT` moves the folio into the swap cache but can\u0027t unmap it, and `MADV_SOFT_OFFLINE` fails with `EBUSY`, while the layouts that don\u0027t merge swap out and migrate normally. When the slices differ as well, I think a later fault in that window can also reuse a slice that is still mapped.\n\nWould you be OK to pick the counting the way `vma_linear_page_index()` does, here or in a separate patch?\n\n```\n\t\tif (!vmg-\u003enext-\u003evm_ops \u0026\u0026 !vmg-\u003enext-\u003evm_file)\n\t\t\treturn vmg-\u003enext-\u003evm_pgoff \u003d\u003d\n\t\t\t       vmg-\u003epgoff + ((vmg-\u003eend - vmg-\u003estart) \u003e\u003e PAGE_SHIFT_4KB);\n```\n\nA /dev/zero `next` then reaches the slice path below, so that path also needs `vmg-\u003eslice_off` rather than `vmg_new_range_slice()`. `vma_slice_off(vmg-\u003enext)` still works there as before, since `vmg-\u003eend \u003d\u003d next-\u003evm_start`.",
