      "writtenOn": "2026-11-01T19:19:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As far as I can see, this stops a faulted private /dev/zero range that `mremap()` moves to another phase, right after its own source, from passing the check against itself and hitting the `VM_BUG_ON_VMA()` below. That can only happen while the prev check doesn\u0027t compare the range\u0027s real slice: B10 skipped it, and `vmg_new_range_slice()` uses the address.\n\nWith `vmg-\u003eslice_off` (see my comment on `vmg_new_range_slice()`), the check compares the object offset of `old_addr` with that of the source\u0027s `vm_end`, or the offset of `old_addr + len` with that of its `vm_start`. Those can\u0027t match, because the moved range lies inside the source. That\u0027s the linearity argument in the comment below.\n\nWould it be OK to drop this hunk and `vma_filter_merge_neighbor()`? `copy_vma()` also runs for native processes and in `!CONFIG_ARM64_PER_PROCESS_PAGE_SIZE` builds, so it would be nice to keep it as upstream wrote it.",
